% Welcome to M Plus Plus!
% The visual math coding language: you write programs in math notation, and Run turns them into real programs.

% Contents
%   1. Files and tabs
%   2. Typing and editing
%   3. The math menu
%   4. Math you can type
%   5. Matrices and cases
%   6. Running a program
%   7. Variables and numbers
%   8. Decisions, loops and functions
%   9. Vectors and matrices
%   10. Sets, sums and logic
%   11. Text, lists, maps and structs
%   12. Graphs and figures

% Rows starting with % are comments: the program skips them, and they are drawn in grey.
% Everything else in this file is a real M++ example. Press Run to run them all, or copy a row into a new file.


% =====  1. Files and tabs  =====

% File, then New makes a new .mpp file; File, then Open File opens one you already have.
% Save with File, then Save File, or ctrl+s.
% Every open file has a tab. A dot on a tab means it has unsaved changes; the cross closes it.
% Drag a tab sideways to reorder your tabs.
% Docs opens this help file.


% =====  2. Typing and editing  =====

% Type as you would in any text editor. Each row is one statement of your program.
% ctrl+c copy, ctrl+x cut, ctrl+v paste, ctrl+a select all, ctrl+z undo, ctrl+y (or ctrl+shift+z) redo.
% The Edit menu has the same commands.
% Hold shift with the arrow keys to select. Click anywhere to move the cursor there.
% Double click a word to select it; triple click to select the whole row.
% Enter keeps the indentation of the row; after a \{ it indents one more level.
% Typing \} on a blank indented row moves it back one level.
% Tab indents (with several rows selected, it indents them all); Shift+Tab moves the row or rows back left.
% Start a row (or the end of one) with % to write a comment; the program skips the rest of the row.
% Backspace next to a math object (a fraction, a matrix...) first marks it in red; press Backspace again to delete it.


% =====  3. The math menu  =====

% Type a backslash to open the math menu, then type part of a name to search it.
% Up and Down choose, Enter or Tab inserts, Escape (or Backspace with an empty search) closes it.
% The names are the usual LaTeX ones, so LaTeX you paste in works too: frac, sqrt, pmatrix, sum, alpha, in, mathbb...
% Two backslashes in a row insert the solve symbol \setminus (see section 9).
% You can also open it from the Insert menu.


% =====  4. Math you can type  =====

% Fractions (frac):   \frac{1}{2}
% Powers: type ^ after something to raise it:   x^{2}   e^{-x}   2^{10}
% Subscripts: type \_ after a name:   x_{1}   v_{max}   A_{2,3}
% Roots (sqrt, sqrt[n]):   \sqrt{2}   \sqrt[3]{27}
% Absolute value, floor, ceiling and norm (abs, floor, ceil, norm):   \abs{-3}   \floor{2.7}   \ceil{2.1}   \norm{v}
% Greek letters are names you can use:   \alpha   \beta   \theta   \pi
% Symbols by their LaTeX names:   \in   \leftarrow   \leq   \neq   \approx   \cdot   \times   \mathbb{R}   \mathbb{N}
% Move between the boxes of a math object with the arrow keys; Right at the end steps out of it.

% These are all usable just how you would in Desmos or Symbolab, and you can assign them to variables:
r := 3
area := \pi \cdot r^{2}


% =====  5. Matrices and cases  =====

% Insert a matrix with pmatrix (round), bmatrix (square), matrix (no brackets) or vmatrix (a determinant).
% In a cell, press Enter to open the insert wheel: an arrow key picks where the new row or column goes, then Enter adds it.
% Backspace at the start of a cell marks its row in red; Tab switches to the column; Backspace again deletes it.
A := \begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\3 & 4\end{bmatrix}

% cases makes a piecewise definition: each row is a value and its condition.
f(x) := \begin{cases}-x & x < 0\\x^{2} & otherwise\end{cases}


% =====  6. Running a program  =====

% Run, then Run compiles your file and runs it. Run, then Stop stops a program that is taking too long.
% What the program prints appears in the terminal at the bottom. Drag the terminal's top edge to resize it.
% Mistakes are underlined in red; hover over one to read what is wrong.
% An error in the terminal can be clicked to jump to the place it is about.
% After the first run, the file is checked again a moment after each change you make.
% Graphs open in their own tabs when the program runs (see section 12).


% =====  7. Variables and numbers  =====

% := makes a new variable, and \leftarrow (leftarrow, or type <-) changes it:
a := 5 + 5
a \leftarrow a + 1
print(a)
% print shows values in the terminal, separated by spaces:
print("a is", a, "and a squared is", a^{2})

% Give a variable a type with \in (in):   n \in \mathbb{N} := 3   x \in \mathbb{R} := 0.5
% \mathbb{N} is whole numbers from 0, \mathbb{Z} all whole numbers, \mathbb{R} real numbers.
% = compares (there is no ==); also \neq, \leq, \geq, \approx, < and >.
% Operators: + - \cdot (or *) / and mod. Writing 2x multiplies, but two names need a \cdot between them.
% / and fractions always give a real number; use \floor{a/b} and mod for whole-number division.
% Built in: sin cos tan ln exp sqrt abs round min max, and the constants \pi, e and \infty.


% =====  8. Decisions, loops and functions  =====

% Blocks go in braces. Type them with the \{ and \} keys:
if a > 5 \{ print("big") \} else \{ print("small") \}
for i \in 1..5 \{ print(i, i^{2}) \}
for t \in 0..0.25..1 \{ print(t) \}
% while and break and continue work as usual.

% A function on one row:
square(x) := x \cdot x
% or with a block and return:
grade(score) \{
    if score \geq 90 \{ return "A" \}
    return "B"
\}
print(square(3), grade(95))


% =====  9. Vectors and matrices  =====

% A one-column matrix is a vector. Vectors and matrices hold real numbers.
M := \begin{pmatrix}2 & 1\\1 & 3\end{pmatrix}
b := \begin{pmatrix}3\\5\end{pmatrix}
% Solve M \cdot x = b with \setminus (two backslashes):
sol := M \setminus b
print(sol, M \cdot sol \approx b)
% Also: M^{-1} (inverse), M^{\top} (transpose), M \cdot N, v \times w (cross), det(M), trace(M), rank(M), eigenvalues(M).
% Pick entries with subscripts, counting from 1:   M_{1,2}   M_{2,*} (row 2)   M_{*,1} (column 1)   b_{1}


% =====  10. Sets, sums and logic  =====

S := \{1, 2, 3, 4\}
print(S \cup \{5\}, S \cap \{2, 9\}, 3 \in S, |S|)
% Build sets from others:   \{x \in S : x > 2\}   \{2k : k \in 1..5\}
% Sums and products (sum, prod) take limits or a set:
print(\sum_{k=1}^{100} k, \prod_{k=1}^{5} k)
% For all and there exists (forall, exists):
print(\forall x \in S : x > 0, \exists x \in S : x > 3)


% =====  11. Text, lists, maps and structs  =====

% Text goes in quotes; $\{...\} puts a value inside it:
name := "Ada"
print("Hello $\{name\}!", |name|)
% Lists use square brackets and push:
xs := [3, 1, 4]
xs.push(1)
print(xs, xs_{2})
% A map looks values up by a key. \mathbb{Z}^{\Sigma *} maps text to whole numbers:
ages \in \mathbb{Z}^{\Sigma *}
ages["Ada"] \leftarrow 36
print(ages["Ada"], "Ada" \in ages)
% A struct (struct, or type < and >) describes a record; a list of them prints as a table:
Person := \begin{angle}name \in \Sigma *\\age \in \mathbb{R}\end{angle}
people := Person*
people.push(<name = "Bob", age = 20>)
print(people)


% =====  12. Graphs and figures  =====

% Insert a graph (graph) and type functions in it, one per line, like y = x^{2}.
% Its title and axis labels are typed in place; the x axis label names the variable.
% The settings button on the graph sets the x and y ranges and switches each axis between linear and log.
\begin{graph}[title=Two curves, xmin=-2, xmax=2]y=x^{2}\\sin(x)\end{graph}
% Each graph opens in a Graph tab when you run the file. Running again updates the tab.
% Data from your program can be plotted too: a line (xs, ys) in a graph draws those points.
% From code, make a Figure and add lines, points, histograms or bars:
fig := Figure<title = "Growth">
fig.Line<x = [1, 2, 3], y = [1, 4, 9], legend = "squares">
